  "reconstructibility": 2,
  "reason": "The records collectively make the later study plausibly reconstructible. R1–R3 already establish molecular-dynamics tensile testing of the closely related equiatomic CoNiCrFeMn FCC alloy over multiple temperatures, while R3 specifically identifies partial-dislocation motion, stacking-fault formation, and local FCC–HCP structural evolution. R15 independently connects low-temperature deformation of Cantor-family alloys to stacking faults, nanotwins, and HCP-like regions. R7, R10, and R14 supply temperature-dependent dislocation-controlled deformation evidence, and R8 shows atomistic high-strain loading at varied temperatures in another FCC HEA. Thus, extending these studies to a bulk FeNiCrMn single crystal and systematically varying orientation and strain rate would be a natural expert proposal. The precise vacancy-cluster nucleation sites, absence of twinning, <-111> strength maximum, and strain-rate-insensitive yield strength are not directly disclosed and would remain results to be discovered."
